WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.666
Tonight, when the sun goes down, you're

00:00:01.666 --> 00:00:02.500
going to flip a switch.

00:00:02.833 --> 00:00:04.458
Light will flood the room and you won't

00:00:04.458 --> 00:00:05.416
think twice about it.

00:00:05.416 --> 00:00:08.541
But for 99.9% of human history, that

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:09.708
switch didn't exist.

00:00:09.958 --> 00:00:11.750
When the sun set, the world went dark.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.125
You couldn't even see your

00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:14.666
own hand in front of your face.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.333
But modern humans almost

00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:17.958
never experienced this.

00:00:17.958 --> 00:00:21.083
For over 300,000 years, every single one

00:00:21.083 --> 00:00:22.208
of your ancestors spent

00:00:22.208 --> 00:00:23.416
roughly half their life

00:00:23.416 --> 00:00:24.708
in near total darkness.

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:25.750
They didn't even have

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.291
candles for most of that time.

00:00:27.458 --> 00:00:29.291
Just the black sky, the stars, and

00:00:29.291 --> 00:00:30.916
whatever fire they could keep alive.

00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:31.708
So what did they

00:00:31.708 --> 00:00:33.208
actually do for all those hours?

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.833
The answer changes everything we think we

00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:36.625
know about sleep,

00:00:37.041 --> 00:00:38.208
storytelling, and what it

00:00:38.208 --> 00:00:39.208
means to be human.

00:00:39.208 --> 00:00:40.416
Let's start with fire.

00:00:40.416 --> 00:00:41.541
Because fire is the reason

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.875
any of this was possible.

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:44.750
The earliest solid evidence of humans

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.833
controlling fire comes from Wonderwerk

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:48.166
Cave in South Africa,

00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:50.000
roughly one million years ago.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.791
But fire didn't just keep people warm.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:52.625
Fire completely

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:54.250
restructured the human day.

00:00:54.541 --> 00:00:56.333
Before fire, our ancestors were like

00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:57.375
every other primate.

00:00:57.583 --> 00:00:59.291
When the sun went down, you climbed a

00:00:59.291 --> 00:01:00.583
tree or found a sheltered

00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:02.041
spot and you stayed still.

00:01:02.041 --> 00:01:03.708
Moving at night meant becoming food.

00:01:04.125 --> 00:01:05.916
Our eyes are terrible in the dark

00:01:05.916 --> 00:01:08.000
compared to the predators that hunted us.

00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:10.791
Leopards, hyenas, wolves, all of them

00:01:10.791 --> 00:01:12.000
could see just fine.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.875
Fire changed that equation overnight.

00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:14.875
Literally.

00:01:14.958 --> 00:01:17.166
A campfire creates a circle of safety

00:01:17.166 --> 00:01:19.083
roughly 30 feet in diameter.

00:01:19.375 --> 00:01:21.583
Inside that circle, predators won't come.

00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:23.708
Outside that circle, you die.

00:01:24.166 --> 00:01:25.666
This is not an exaggeration.

00:01:26.083 --> 00:01:27.791
Anthropologists studying modern

00:01:27.791 --> 00:01:29.583
hunter-gatherer groups have documented

00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:30.500
that people who wander

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.000
away from the fire at night are

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.166
significantly more likely

00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:34.291
to be killed by predators.

00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:35.291
Even today.

00:01:35.291 --> 00:01:36.583
But here's what's fascinating.

00:01:37.083 --> 00:01:39.208
Fire didn't just protect humans at night.

00:01:39.208 --> 00:01:40.000
It gave them something

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.458
they'd never had before.

00:01:41.958 --> 00:01:42.458
Extra hours.

00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:43.500
Think about this.

00:01:44.041 --> 00:01:44.666
Before fire, the

00:01:44.666 --> 00:01:46.250
useful day ended at sunset.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.916
That's maybe 12 hours in

00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:49.666
summer, as few as 8 in winter.

00:01:49.666 --> 00:01:51.333
Fire suddenly added 2-4

00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:53.208
usable hours to every single day.

00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:55.000
But over thousands of generations, those

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.625
extra hours changed our species.

00:01:57.041 --> 00:01:59.958
In 2014, anthropologist Polly Wiesner

00:01:59.958 --> 00:02:01.375
published a study in the

00:02:01.375 --> 00:02:02.666
Proceedings of the National

00:02:02.666 --> 00:02:03.791
Academy of Sciences.

00:02:04.208 --> 00:02:06.125
She spent years recording conversations

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:06.625
among the

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:09.291
Jew-slash-Hwan-see-Bushman of the Kalahari

00:02:09.291 --> 00:02:11.000
Desert, one of the last groups on Earth

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.000
still living something

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.291
close to the original human

00:02:13.375 --> 00:02:13.750
lifestyle.

00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:15.250
She found was striking.

00:02:15.791 --> 00:02:18.000
During the day, about 80% of

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.250
conversations were practical.

00:02:19.708 --> 00:02:20.500
Who's hunting where?

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:21.541
Which plants are ready?

00:02:22.166 --> 00:02:24.625
Plants, logistics, economic negotiations.

00:02:25.083 --> 00:02:26.125
Daytime talk was work.

00:02:26.375 --> 00:02:28.416
But at night around the fire, the entire

00:02:28.416 --> 00:02:30.291
character of conversation changed.

00:02:30.541 --> 00:02:32.083
At night, 81% of

00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:33.416
conversations were stories.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.458
Tales of people in distant places.

00:02:35.791 --> 00:02:36.208
Adventures.

00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:38.291
Myths about the origins of the world.

00:02:38.708 --> 00:02:40.291
Jokes that made the whole camp laugh.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:42.083
Wiesner argued that fire-like

00:02:42.083 --> 00:02:43.208
conversation is where

00:02:43.208 --> 00:02:44.375
human culture was born.

00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:46.291
The campfire was the original theater,

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:47.208
the original

00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:49.125
classroom, the original church.

00:02:49.166 --> 00:02:51.291
It's where humans learned to think beyond

00:02:51.291 --> 00:02:52.375
the immediate present

00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:53.541
and imagine things that

00:02:53.541 --> 00:02:54.500
don't exist yet.

00:02:54.833 --> 00:02:56.041
It's where we became us.

00:02:56.458 --> 00:02:57.416
But the night wasn't just

00:02:57.416 --> 00:02:58.875
about stories around the fire.

00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:00.500
There was something else happening,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.875
something that scientists only

00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:03.041
rediscovered in the last

00:03:03.041 --> 00:03:03.916
30 years.

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:05.708
You were not designed to sleep the way

00:03:05.708 --> 00:03:06.791
you sleep right now.

00:03:06.791 --> 00:03:09.416
In 2001, historian Roger E. Kirch

00:03:09.416 --> 00:03:10.500
published a paper that

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.708
changed sleep science.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.833
He had spent 16 years digging through

00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:15.500
historical records, and he found

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.666
something nobody expected.

00:03:17.166 --> 00:03:19.958
Over 500 references, spanning centuries,

00:03:20.291 --> 00:03:21.083
prayer books from the

00:03:21.083 --> 00:03:23.000
1400s, court records,

00:03:23.458 --> 00:03:25.541
diaries, medical texts, letters from

00:03:25.541 --> 00:03:26.833
ordinary people, even

00:03:26.833 --> 00:03:28.208
passages in Homer's Odyssey.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.000
All of them mentioned the same thing.

00:03:30.291 --> 00:03:32.166
First sleep and second sleep.

00:03:32.166 --> 00:03:34.166
Before the Industrial Age, people did not

00:03:34.166 --> 00:03:36.333
sleep in one continuous block.

00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:38.208
They went to bed shortly after sunset,

00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:40.791
slept for about four hours, then woke up.

00:03:40.791 --> 00:03:42.583
They stayed awake for one to two hours in

00:03:42.583 --> 00:03:43.291
the middle of the night.

00:03:43.541 --> 00:03:45.333
Then they went back to sleep for another

00:03:45.333 --> 00:03:46.500
four hours until dawn.

00:03:46.958 --> 00:03:47.875
This wasn't insomnia.

00:03:48.291 --> 00:03:49.000
This was normal.

00:03:49.291 --> 00:03:51.083
This was how every human on the planet

00:03:51.083 --> 00:03:52.583
slept for thousands of years.

00:03:52.875 --> 00:03:54.041
And what did people do during that

00:03:54.041 --> 00:03:55.125
wakeful period in the

00:03:55.125 --> 00:03:55.833
middle of the night?

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:56.583
Everything.

00:03:57.041 --> 00:03:58.750
A 16th century French physician

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.166
recommended that couples conceive

00:04:00.166 --> 00:04:01.000
children during this

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.875
window because they would do it better.

00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:04.750
People visited neighbors, they

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:05.708
interpreted their dreams

00:04:05.708 --> 00:04:06.791
while they were still fresh.

00:04:07.125 --> 00:04:09.083
They wrote, "They simply lay in bed in

00:04:09.083 --> 00:04:09.833
the dark and thought."

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:12.500
A doctor's manual from the 1500s advised

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.541
patients that the best

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:14.916
time to study and reflect

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.625
was during the "watch" as they called it.

00:04:17.625 --> 00:04:19.625
The quiet hours between first and second

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:20.875
sleep when the mind was

00:04:20.875 --> 00:04:22.458
calm and the world was still.

00:04:22.791 --> 00:04:24.458
Now historians had been reading these

00:04:24.458 --> 00:04:26.250
references for centuries, but

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.291
they kept translating "first

00:04:28.375 --> 00:04:29.833
sleep" as something else.

00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:31.708
Because they couldn't imagine that people

00:04:31.708 --> 00:04:33.791
ever slept differently than we do now.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.750
The assumption was so deep that

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.000
translators literally mistranslated

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.291
ancient Greek and medieval

00:04:39.333 --> 00:04:40.500
Latin to make it fit

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.541
their modern experience.

00:04:41.875 --> 00:04:43.583
But then science confirmed what history

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:45.000
had been saying all along.

00:04:45.166 --> 00:04:48.458
In 1992, psychiatrist Thomas Ware at the

00:04:48.458 --> 00:04:49.500
National Institute of

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:50.583
Mental Health ran an

00:04:50.583 --> 00:04:51.083
experiment.

00:04:51.416 --> 00:04:53.333
He took a group of volunteers and removed

00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:55.208
all artificial light from their lives.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.000
For one month, they experienced 14 hours

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.333
of darkness every night,

00:04:59.333 --> 00:05:00.250
just like our ancestors

00:05:00.375 --> 00:05:01.500
would have before fire

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.416
extended the evening.

00:05:02.625 --> 00:05:04.583
Within weeks, every single participant

00:05:04.583 --> 00:05:06.125
settled into the same pattern.

00:05:06.541 --> 00:05:08.625
Four hours of sleep, one to three hours

00:05:08.625 --> 00:05:09.541
of quiet wakefulness,

00:05:09.958 --> 00:05:11.416
then four more hours of

00:05:11.416 --> 00:05:11.625
sleep.

00:05:11.875 --> 00:05:13.208
Nobody told them to do this.

00:05:13.208 --> 00:05:14.208
Nobody suggested it.

00:05:14.583 --> 00:05:16.208
Nobody's just did it, as if some ancient

00:05:16.208 --> 00:05:16.958
program had been

00:05:16.958 --> 00:05:18.250
waiting inside them the whole

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:19.583
time buried under

00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:21.166
centuries of artificial light.

00:05:21.625 --> 00:05:23.166
Ware measured their hormone levels during

00:05:23.166 --> 00:05:24.291
the wakeful period and

00:05:24.291 --> 00:05:25.375
found something remarkable.

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:27.208
The participants were in a state unlike

00:05:27.208 --> 00:05:28.958
any normal waking state.

00:05:28.958 --> 00:05:30.500
Their brains were producing elevated

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.000
levels of prolactin, the

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.166
same hormone released during

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:34.791
meditation and after orgasm.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.708
They described feeling peaceful,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.000
reflective, almost meditative.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.041
It wasn't just a gap in sleep, it was its

00:05:41.041 --> 00:05:42.666
own distinct state of consciousness.

00:05:43.041 --> 00:05:43.833
One that most modern

00:05:43.833 --> 00:05:45.458
humans have never experienced.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:46.583
So what killed it?

00:05:47.166 --> 00:05:48.958
The answer is simpler than you'd think.

00:05:49.333 --> 00:05:49.541
Light.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:51.291
In the 1600s, cities

00:05:51.291 --> 00:05:52.958
began installing street lamps.

00:05:53.375 --> 00:05:55.000
Paris was one of the first, under Louis

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.000
XIV, who wanted to reduce crime.

00:05:57.291 --> 00:05:58.000
London followed.

00:05:58.208 --> 00:05:59.666
The night, which had been a place of

00:05:59.666 --> 00:06:02.416
danger and fear, slowly became navigable.

00:06:02.416 --> 00:06:03.750
Then came cheaper candles.

00:06:04.041 --> 00:06:06.250
Then gas lamps in the 1800s.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.875
Then in 1879, Thomas Edison

00:06:08.875 --> 00:06:10.916
commercialized the electric light bulb.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.750
And within a generation, the night as

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:13.875
humans had known it for

00:06:13.875 --> 00:06:15.583
300,000 years was gone.

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.750
And here's the part nobody talks about.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.083
The word curfew comes

00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:20.875
from the French couvre-feur.

00:06:20.875 --> 00:06:22.583
It literally means "cover fire."

00:06:22.583 --> 00:06:24.458
In medieval towns, a bell would ring at

00:06:24.458 --> 00:06:25.083
night, signaling

00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:26.916
everyone to cover or extinguish

00:06:26.916 --> 00:06:27.416
their fires.

00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:29.541
Not because of safety, because night was

00:06:29.541 --> 00:06:30.458
supposed to be dark.

00:06:30.791 --> 00:06:31.666
Night was when you slept.

00:06:32.208 --> 00:06:33.541
Night was when you were vulnerable.

00:06:33.833 --> 00:06:34.875
For most of history, being

00:06:34.875 --> 00:06:36.083
out at night was suspicious.

00:06:36.583 --> 00:06:38.583
In many medieval European cities, walking

00:06:38.583 --> 00:06:39.625
outside after dark

00:06:39.625 --> 00:06:41.125
without a lantern was literally

00:06:41.291 --> 00:06:41.708
illegal.

00:06:42.041 --> 00:06:42.833
Night belonged to

00:06:42.833 --> 00:06:44.125
criminals and the supernatural.

00:06:44.583 --> 00:06:46.166
People feared it in a way that's almost

00:06:46.166 --> 00:06:47.958
impossible for us to understand today.

00:06:48.208 --> 00:06:49.291
And then we conquered it.

00:06:49.291 --> 00:06:50.333
We flooded it with light.

00:06:50.583 --> 00:06:51.791
And we thought that was progress.

00:06:52.083 --> 00:06:53.125
But here's what we lost.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.291
Modern humans are exposed to artificial

00:06:55.291 --> 00:06:56.500
light an average of seven

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.000
to eight hours after sunset.

00:06:58.333 --> 00:07:00.041
This light, especially the blue light

00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:00.583
from screens,

00:07:00.916 --> 00:07:02.250
suppresses your body's production

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:03.250
of melatonin, the

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.750
hormone that regulates sleep.

00:07:04.833 --> 00:07:05.875
Your body doesn't know the difference

00:07:05.875 --> 00:07:07.250
between a light bulb and the sun.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.791
When light hits your retina at 11 p.m.,

00:07:09.791 --> 00:07:11.458
your brain interprets it as daytime.

00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:13.250
It delays melatonin release.

00:07:13.583 --> 00:07:14.625
It fragments your sleep.

00:07:14.958 --> 00:07:16.708
It disrupts the circadian rhythm that

00:07:16.708 --> 00:07:18.166
evolved over millions of years.

00:07:18.583 --> 00:07:19.958
Researchers have found that people who go

00:07:19.958 --> 00:07:21.000
camping for just one

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.125
week with no artificial

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.500
light at all have their melatonin cycles

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:25.958
reset by nearly two hours.

00:07:26.291 --> 00:07:28.208
Their bodies start producing melatonin at

00:07:28.208 --> 00:07:30.166
sunset instead of hours after it.

00:07:30.166 --> 00:07:32.208
That's all it takes for 300,000 years of

00:07:32.208 --> 00:07:33.416
programming to reassert itself.

00:07:33.875 --> 00:07:35.041
We didn't just change when we sleep.

00:07:35.333 --> 00:07:36.750
We eliminated an entire

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.208
phase of human consciousness.

00:07:38.583 --> 00:07:41.083
That quiet reflective state between first

00:07:41.083 --> 00:07:42.250
and second sleep, the

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:43.500
state that wears subjects

00:07:43.583 --> 00:07:45.458
described as peaceful and meditative,

00:07:45.458 --> 00:07:47.458
doesn't exist anymore for most people.

00:07:47.458 --> 00:07:48.583
Instead, we have insomnia.

00:07:48.958 --> 00:07:49.958
And we treat it like a disorder.

00:07:50.208 --> 00:07:51.791
But what if it's not a disorder at all?

00:07:51.791 --> 00:07:53.750
What if waking up at 2 a.m. and lying

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:54.791
there in the dark isn't

00:07:54.791 --> 00:07:56.000
your body malfunctioning?

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:56.833
What if it's your body

00:07:56.833 --> 00:07:58.458
remembering what it was built to do?

00:07:58.458 --> 00:08:01.000
For 300,000 years, your ancestors spent

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:01.708
their nights in a

00:08:01.708 --> 00:08:03.041
rhythm that modern science is

00:08:03.083 --> 00:08:04.541
only beginning to understand.

00:08:04.833 --> 00:08:06.375
They gathered around fires and told

00:08:06.375 --> 00:08:07.833
stories that built civilizations.

00:08:08.333 --> 00:08:10.416
They slept in two phases with a window of

00:08:10.416 --> 00:08:11.875
quiet reflection in between.

00:08:12.208 --> 00:08:14.291
They lived in darkness so complete that

00:08:14.291 --> 00:08:15.375
the Milky Way wasn't

00:08:15.375 --> 00:08:16.333
something you had to drive

00:08:16.333 --> 00:08:17.791
to a national park to see.

00:08:17.791 --> 00:08:18.666
It was just the sky.

00:08:18.916 --> 00:08:20.291
And every night when they woke in the

00:08:20.291 --> 00:08:21.500
dark between first and

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:22.833
second sleep, they lay there

00:08:22.833 --> 00:08:24.583
in silence and thought about their lives,

00:08:24.958 --> 00:08:26.083
their dreams, and the

00:08:26.083 --> 00:08:26.750
world they would wake

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:27.500
up to in the morning.

00:08:27.791 --> 00:08:29.541
We traded all of that for a light switch.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:30.583
And most of us never

00:08:30.583 --> 00:08:31.458
even knew it was gone.

